cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "DORA Attestation Gatekeeper"
abstract: >-
  Reference implementation of a supervisory gatekeeper for HSM attestation
  verification under DORA. Operated by the National Competent Authority
  (from 1.7.0 also inside a regulated organisation, profile org), the
  gatekeeper executes a two-step verify/confirm protocol with the
  financial entity, signs receipts that are independently verifiable
  against pinned vendor trust anchors (Securosys, Yubico, Thales Luna,
  Crypto4A, Fortanix, Entrust nShield, and Marvell/Cavium for Marvell
  LiquidSecurity, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud HSM), maintains a hash-chained tamper-evident audit
  log, and enforces NCA-role-based authorisation. Version 1.2.0 added a
  settlement-time signature verification endpoint (POST /api/v1/verify)
  for use by settlement-rail enforcement components such as railgate.
  Version 1.4.0 fixes a start-up failure in 1.3.0 (duplicate key in
  application.yaml), moves the receipt wire format to v2 so that the
  confirmation nonce is inside the signed form (lockstep upgrade with hsm
  1.4.0 required), binds Step 7 confirmation to the jurisdiction in the
  path and to the mTLS principal that performed the verification, consumes
  the nonce atomically, makes settlement compliance follow the
  confirmation outcome, and caches chain-integrity checks behind a
  supervisor-only health endpoint. Version 1.5.0 makes the settlement
  contract with railgate work end to end by storing the certificate
  confirmed at Step 7 and resolving it from serial number and issuer DN
  (lockstep upgrade with hsm 1.5.0 and railgate 1.5.0 required), reads
  YubiHSM capabilities big-endian and rejects re-imported keys, makes every
  DORA article bit depend on a valid attestation signature, builds
  issuer-CA paths through intermediate certificates, and verifies audit-log
  integrity against the file on disk with the configured algorithm and
  retired keys. Version 1.6.0 keeps the submitted attestation evidence in
  the registry, records and signs the customer and the technical supplier
  (receipt form v3; lockstep upgrade with hsm 1.6.0 required), signs every
  Step 7 confirmation response, checks certificate validity at settlement,
  admits only RSA-4096 keys under the default key policy, adds Marvell
  LiquidSecurity, Thales Luna, Crypto4A, Fortanix and Entrust nShield as
  vendors (nine in all), and detects every PIT mutant of its production
  code. Version 1.7.0 records the key-lifecycle events signed, rotated,
  revoked and decommissioned in the audit log, gives each audit entry the
  key's inventory fields and a per-key chain, signs every settlement
  verdict, implements the triangulation of bank reports, the operator's
  report and the registry, adds the organisation-internal profile, lets
  the log-signing key be held in an HSM and attested at start-up, adds
  RFC 3161 time-stamping of entries or of the chain head and a COSE_Sign1
  export, reports YubiHSM 2 firmware and FIPS state, records each pinned
  trust anchor's manufacturer source (each confirmed against the
  manufacturer's material), and lets the
  operator replace a pinned anchor with a root obtained from the
  manufacturer; the HSM and
  time-stamping paths are tested only with a test JCA provider and a local
  time-stamping service. PIT detects every mutant of the 1.7.0 code as well.
  Its audit log is one of the four data sources of the data-minimised
  quadruple-triangulation model: at settlement time only digest,
  signature, and certificate identifiers traverse the supervisor boundary
  — transaction payload content is never exposed to the supervisor. Companions: hsm (financial-entity-side HSM
  attestation verification) and railgate (central-bank settlement-rail
  enforcement).
